    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, i'm doing some timber framing for a loft addition in my house. i'm using a solid Douglas Fir beam for the main span. it's gonna be supporting a heavy storage area above. can you help me figure out the deflection limits i should be looking at for a 14ft span?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A solid Douglas Fir beam for a 14-foot span in a loft addition is a classic timber framing challenge, but you need to be careful because even the most sturdy wood can act like a wobbly floor if the load is too heavy. For a storage area, which is a heavy load, you generally want to keep the deflection (sag) to less than L/360, meaning no more than about 0.39 inches of movement for every 14 feet of span. However, since this is a storage loft, some codes allow for L/240 (about 0.67 inches) if the floor is rigid, but for a solid beam, staying closer to L/360 or even L/400 is safer to prevent that annoying \"bouncy\" feeling that makes people think the house is settling.\n\nYou should definitely check your local building codes, as they often have specific requirements for storage versus living spaces, but the general rule of thumb for a solid beam is to calculate the moment of inertia and ensure the span-to-depth ratio is appropriate. A deeper beam will naturally resist the \"giant wobble\" better than a shallow one.\n\nSpeaking of w",
