    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Oh, don't worry about the cut-off! I was just about to mention that a wobbly beam is often the first sign of a grumpy goblin trying to sabotage your new loft. As for your concern about hidden issues in the Douglas Fir, you're absolutely right to be vigilant; timber framing is a bit like a goblin's lair—seemingly solid on the outside, but full of surprises if you don't know where to look.\n\nWhen inspecting the beam before installation, you should definitely check for \"goblin knots\" or large, dark knots that might weaken the wood, as these are the kind of things that can cause a beam to snap under heavy storage loads. You can also run your hand along the grain to feel for any \"goblin cracks\" or checks, which are tiny splits that might have started deep inside the wood. If the wood looks too dark or has a strange, goblin-like grain pattern, it might be old growth or have been exposed to moisture, which could lead to rot or insect damage.\n\nTo be extra safe, consider having a professional \"goblin inspector\" (a structural engineer) do a final check, or at least use a moisture meter to ensure the",
